  "title": "AVL Eagle / ShowPro Multi-Image Controller",
  "subtitle": "A CP/M computer that choreographed thirty slide projectors with cue lists and proprietary timecode",
  "year": "1980",
  "era": "1976-1992",
  "category": "Presentation & Performance Control",
  "blurb": "Before PowerPoint there was multi-image: nine, twelve, thirty 35mm slide projectors aimed at one wide screen, cross-fading until photographs seemed to move. The AVL Eagle (1980) was the first microcomputer built to program that spectacle. Its PROCALL language wrote a timed cue list — Advance, Alt, Cut, Dissolve, Freeze, Loop — that drove up to thirty Kodak Ektagraphic projectors, five film projectors, and twenty auxiliary control points. Commands left the machine as two audio-frequency telemetry streams called PosiTrak down ordinary RCA cables into outboard boxes named Doves, each of which steered three projectors; the whole list chased a biphase 'ClockTrak' timecode stripe on a multitrack audio tape. The programmer sat with headphones on, listening to the soundtrack and pressing keys in time, effectively performing the show into memory. It is the museum's clearest example of an interface whose output medium is neither screen nor paper but a room full of physical machines — and of a whole profession, the multi-image programmer, that desktop presentation software erased.",
  "creator": "Audio Visual Laboratories (AVL), of New Jersey, US; later spun off as Eagle Computer, Inc.",
  "manufacturer": "Audio Visual Laboratories / Eagle Computer, Inc.",
  "tagline": "Cue lists for a roomful of projectors",
